legend:
Bao is the on-call engineer of a storage node on the university cluster, and its front line is the humblest component of all: an \textbf{RLU cache}. It stores key--value pairs with a fixed capacity of $n$ pairs. At any moment the pairs in the cache have a \emph{recency order}: the most recently used pair has order $1$, the next one has order $2$, and so on; the pair with the largest order is the \emph{least recently used} one, and it is evicted first when space runs out.

Initially the cache is full: it contains exactly the keys $1, 2, \ldots, n$, each with value $0$, inserted in this order --- so key $1$ is initially the least recently used key (order $n$) and key $n$ is the most recently used one (order $1$).

You have to process $q$ queries of two types:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{1 a b} --- put key $a$ with value $b$ into the cache:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item if key $a$ is already in the cache, its value is replaced by $b$ and $a$ becomes the most recently used key;
      \item otherwise the least recently used key is \emph{discarded} (invalidated) from the cache, and $a$ with value $b$ is inserted as the most recently used key.
    \end{itemize}
  \item \texttt{2 a} --- look up key $a$:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item if key $a$ is in the cache, print its value and its current order $r$ ($r = 1$ means most recently used, $r = n$ means least recently used); afterwards $a$ becomes the most recently used key;
      \item otherwise print $-1$ (the key was never inserted or has already been invalidated).
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

output:
For each query of type $2$ (in the order the queries appear, across all test cases), print one line:

\begin{itemize}
  \item if key $a$ is in the cache, print two integers $b$ and $r$ --- its current value and its current order;
  \item otherwise print the single integer $-1$.
\end{itemize}

input:
The first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 2 \cdot 10^5$) --- the number of test cases.

The first line of each test case contains two integers $n$ and $q$ ($1 \le n, q \le 2 \cdot 10^5$) --- the capacity of the cache and the number of queries.

The next $q$ lines describe the queries in the order they must be processed. Each line has one of two forms:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{1 a b} ($1 \le a \le 10^9$, $1 \le b \le 10^9$) --- put key $a$ with value $b$ into the cache;
  \item \texttt{2 a} ($1 \le a \le 10^9$) --- look up key $a$.
\end{itemize}

It is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $2 \cdot 10^5$, and the sum of $q$ over all test cases does not exceed $2 \cdot 10^5$.

tutorial:
Simulate the process with the right data structures.

Give every pair in the cache a timestamp: the index of the last operation that touched it. Initially assign timestamps $1, 2, \ldots, n$ to keys $1, 2, \ldots, n$, and keep a global counter that is incremented by every insertion, update, or successful lookup. A key with a larger timestamp is more recently used, so for a key with timestamp $ts$ its order equals (the number of active timestamps greater than $ts$) $+ 1$.

Maintain three structures, all keyed by timestamp or key:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a hash map from key to $(\text{value}, \text{timestamp})$;
  \item a Fenwick tree over the timestamp range $1 \ldots n+q$ counting, for each timestamp, whether it is currently active (this answers "how many active timestamps are $\le ts$", from which the order follows);
  \item a \texttt{std::set} of pairs $(\text{timestamp}, \text{key})$, whose minimum element is the least recently used key --- exactly what must be evicted.
\end{itemize}

Each query touches a constant number of elements of each structure, so it costs $O(\log(n+q))$. The total complexity is $O((n+q) \log (n+q))$ per test case.

An $O(n)$-per-query simulation (e.g. keeping pairs in a vector and scanning / shifting it) is hopelessly slow: with $n = q = 2 \cdot 10^5$ it needs up to $4 \cdot 10^{10}$ elementary operations.

A common mistake is to forget that a successful type-2 lookup also counts as \emph{using} the key: it must refresh the key to most recently used, otherwise the key can be discarded too early.
